  "response": "I’ll run the scan and inspect the three modules to separate actionable contract problems from naming heuristics, then propose a refactor plan without changing code.\n\nRan `crimes scan --format json` and inspected the three modules. The live scan reports **76 findings**, versus 74 in the supplied report; IDs have shifted, so locations and symbols are the better references. No code changed.\n\nThe requested cluster contains **eight live findings**: four naming findings, one options-bag finding, and three overlapping Svelte return-shape findings.\n\n**`name_behavior_mismatch` — clarify when effects happen**\n\n- `src/rollup.ts:38–159`, `getRollupConfig`: rename to `prepareDtsRollupConfig`, or separate environment loading and logger configuration from configuration assembly. It currently calls `setSilent` and mutates the caller’s `options.dts.entry`. Copy and normalize declaration settings instead. Its cleanup happens later through a registered hook; merely requesting the configuration does **not** delete files.\n- `src/rollup.ts:77–81`, `buildStart`: retain this required Rollup hook name. Extract its body into `cleanDeclarationOutputs` so the effect is explicit.\n- `src/index.ts:167–459`, exported `build`: retain the established public API name, add an explicit `Promise<void>` return type, and document that it writes outputs, optionally cleans, starts workers/watchers, and runs success hooks.\n- `src/index.ts:291–365`, `buildAll`: rename the internal helper to `runBuildCycle`; extract cleanup and success-hook management.\n\n**Agent risk today:** an agent could reuse configuration preparation as a harmless read, overlook mutations to shared options, or invoke a build cycle without accounting for cleanup and child-process termination. The scanner’s assumption that “build” implies purity is weak; extracting effects is more useful than renaming the public API.\n\n**`option_bag_junk_drawer` — narrow internal ownership**\n\n- `src/rollup.ts:38–159`, `getRollupConfig`: it already accepts the named `NormalizedOptions` type, so the reported “implicit shape” is misleading. The actual problem is that a declaration builder receives the entire application configuration.\n- Introduce focused internal records for declaration inputs, output settings, and cleanup policy. Pass package metadata, dependencies, and logging context separately.\n- Build an explicit serializable `DtsWorkerRequest` in `src/index.ts`, replacing the spread of all options followed by a growing list of fields set to `undefined`.\n- Give `normalizeOptions` an explicit `Promise<NormalizedOptions>` contract and construct required fields without relying on the final assertion. Distinguish raw JSON compiler settings from parsed `ts.CompilerOptions`.\n\n**Agent risk today:** a new callback option can accidentally enter the worker message and fail structured cloning. Agents can also assume normalization guarantees fields that the implementation only asserts are present. Keep the broad public `Options` API compatible while narrowing internal boundaries.\n\n**`return_shape_roulette` — separate callback contracts**\n\nThe live scan flags `sveltePlugin` (`9–124`), `setup` (`17–122`), and the anonymous load callback (`31–121`). These overlap: the detector aggregates returns from nested callbacks. `sveltePlugin` already returns `Plugin`; `setup` itself does not return six variants.\n\nExtract and explicitly type:\n\n| Helper | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| `resolveSvelteCss` | esbuild `OnResolveResult` |\n| `loadSvelteComponent` | `Promise<OnLoadResult>` |\n| `preprocessTypeScriptScript` | Svelte’s script-preprocessor contract |\n| `toEsbuildDiagnostic` | esbuild `PartialMessage` |\n\nReplace diagnostic `any` with a named Svelte message shape; narrow caught `unknown` errors before conversion. Preserve legitimate load success (`contents`, `warnings`) and failure (`errors`) results. Use a discriminated union only if introducing a separate internal compilation result.\n\n**Agent risk today:** an agent could confuse preprocess `{ code, map }` results with esbuild `{ contents }` results, assume every diagnostic has source positions, or “standardize” unrelated callback returns into an invalid shared shape.\n\n**Supporting findings and implementation order**\n\nThe same files also have large-function/file findings. Extract normalization, declaration-worker orchestration, build-cycle execution, watcher handling, and Svelte compilation along those responsibility boundaries. Connectivity findings warrant checking callers, rather than removing imports to lower a score.\n\nBefore extraction, add focused regression coverage for cleanup preserving declarations, repeated builds leaving input options unchanged, worker-message serialization, and Svelte success/error/preprocessing branches. Also cover Rollup failure signaling: `handleError(error)` already preserves error information and posts an error, but `runRollup` then resolves, allowing `startRollup` to post success afterward. Make that completion contract unambiguous.\n\nImplement types and boundary adapters first, extract helpers second, then run the project build, relevant tests, and scan again. Assess preserved behavior alongside the findings count.",
